{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Game Scene.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Game Scene.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"game scene game scene","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Game Scene.md","fileName":"Game Scene.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Game Scene","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 15:28\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Level and Scene Design]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Level and Scene Design","Game Pacing","Game Verb","Game Part","agamedesignvocabulary.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Game Scene.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Game Scene.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"game scene game scene","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Game Scene.md","fileName":"Game Scene.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Game Scene","heading":"Game Scene","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A game scene is the smallest useful unit of [[Game Pacing|pacing]] in which a distinct development of rules or play occurs. Depending on the game, a scene may be called a level, stage, round, wave, board, mission, or screen, but those labels do not determine its actual boundaries.\n\nA scene arranges [[Game Verb|verbs]] and [[Game Part|objects]] so the player can encounter, understand, and apply particular relationships. One conventional level may contain several scenes when its focus or choice structure changes.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Level and Scene Design","Game Pacing","Game Verb","Game Part","agamedesignvocabulary.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Game Scene.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Game Scene.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"game scene game scene","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Game Scene.md","fileName":"Game Scene.md","folder":"6 - Full Not